.TH AJAZZCTL 1 2026-10-13 "aj179-linux" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ajazzctl \- read and change the settings of an Ajazz AJ179 mouse
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ajazzctl
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ajazzctl
talks to an Ajazz AJ179 (dual mode) mouse and offers the settings of the
official Windows driver: report rate, DPI stages and their colours, lighting,
lift-off distance, Motion Sync, Angle Snap, power options, button mapping and
macros.
.PP
It uses the USB cable when the mouse is plugged in and the 2.4\ GHz receiver
otherwise.
Both links reach the same settings; the mouse keeps one set for both.
.PP
Every setting is written to the mouse itself, so it stays after a reboot or on
another computer.
After each change
.B ajazzctl
reads the setting back from the mouse and prints
.B ok (verified by read-back)
only if it matches.
Buttons and macros are the exception: the mouse has no command to read them
back, so only its acknowledgement is checked.
.SS Waking the mouse
Over the receiver, settings only reach the mouse while it is awake and in use.
If it is asleep or idle,
.B ajazzctl
prints
.B mouse is asleep - move it
(or
.BR idle )
and waits up to five minutes for the mouse to move, then retries.
Over the cable the mouse is always awake.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Information
.TP
.B info
Device id, sensor, which link is used, and the battery level (marked
.B charging
on the cable, or
.B last reported
while the mouse sleeps).
This works even while the mouse sleeps.
.TP
.BR show " [" \-\-json ]
All current settings, read from the mouse.
With
.BR \-\-json ,
they are printed as one JSON line, for the GUI and scripts.
The mouse cannot report its buttons or macros, so those are what ajazzctl last wrote.
.SS Pointer
.TP
.BI rate " hz"
Report (polling) rate:
.BR 125 ", " 250 ", " 500 " or " 1000 .
.TP
.BI dpi " stage value"
Set the DPI of
.I stage
(1\(en6).
Values are rounded down to a multiple of 50 and kept within the sensor's range
(50\(en26000 for the AJ179's PAW3395).
.TP
.BI "dpi select" " stage"
Make
.I stage
the active one.
.TP
.BI dpi-colour " stage #RRGGBB"
LED colour shown for
.I stage
(1\(en6).
.SS Lighting
.TP
.B lighting off
.TQ
.BR "lighting breathing " [ \fIbrightness speed\fR ]
.TQ
.BR "lighting constant " [ \fI#RRGGBB\fR "] [" \fIbrightness speed\fR ]
Lighting effect.
.I brightness
and
.I speed
are 0\(en4; when left out the current values are kept.
.B constant
uses red when no colour is given.
.B breathing
cycles through the seven fixed colours the official driver uses.
.SS Sensor
.TP
.BR "sensor lod " 1 | 2
Lift-off distance: 1\ mm or 2\ mm.
.TP
.BR "sensor motion-sync " on | off
.TQ
.BR "sensor angle-snap " on | off
.SS Power
.TP
.BR "power wake-on-move " on | off
.TQ
.BR "power lights-off-on-move " on | off
.TP
.BI "power sleep" " steps"
Sleep after this many 10\ s steps (1\(en255).
The official app offers 1, 3, 6, 12, 30, 60, 120 and 180
(10\ s, 30\ s, 1, 2, 5, 10, 20 and 30 minutes); the factory value is 30 (5 minutes).
.TP
.BI "power debounce" " ms"
Button debounce in milliseconds, 0\(en20 as in the official app; the factory value is 8.
.SS Buttons
Buttons are numbered
.B 1
left,
.B 2
right,
.B 3
middle,
.B 4
back (rear side button),
.B 5
forward (front side button),
.B 6
DPI.
Button 1 cannot be remapped, so the mouse can always be used to undo a mistake.
.TP
.BI button " number function"
Give button 2\(en6 a new
.IR function :
.RS
.TP
.BR left ", " right ", " middle ", " back ", " forward
mouse buttons
.TP
.BR scroll-up ", " scroll-down
one wheel step
.TP
.B dpi-loop
cycle through the DPI stages (factory function of button 6)
.TP
.BR dpi-up ", " dpi-down
next or previous DPI stage
.TP
.BR play-pause ", " stop ", " previous ", " next ", " volume-up ", " volume-down ", " mute
media keys
.TP
.BI key: combination
a keyboard key with optional modifiers, e.g.
.BR key:ctrl+c ,
.BR key:super+tab ,
.BR key:f5 .
Modifiers:
.BR ctrl ", " shift ", " alt ", " super .
Keys:
.BR a \(en z ,
.BR 0 \(en 9 ,
.BR f1 \(en f12 ,
.BR enter ", " esc ", " tab ", " space ", " backspace .
.RE
.TP
.B buttons reset
Restore the factory mapping.
.SS Macros
.TP
.BI macro " number token" ...
Store a macro and make button
.I number
(2\(en6) play it once per press.
Each
.I token
is either a key combination in the same form as
.B key:
above but without the prefix, which is pressed and released, or
.BI wait: ms
to pause after the previous key (1\(en65535 ms; 10 ms otherwise).
A macro holds up to 101 key events; a key with two modifiers uses six.
.SS Diagnostics
.TP
.BI read " cmd"
Send one raw read command and print the 32-byte reply in hex.
Only the known read commands are accepted:
.BR 10 ", " 12 ", " 13 ", " 14 ", " 15 ", " 16 ", " 17 ", " 20 .
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ajazzctl show
ajazzctl dpi 2 1600
ajazzctl dpi select 2
ajazzctl lighting constant \(aq#00ffff\(aq 2 0
ajazzctl sensor motion-sync on
ajazzctl button 4 key:ctrl+c
ajazzctl macro 5 ctrl+a wait:50 ctrl+c
ajazzctl buttons reset
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/ajazzctl/buttons
The last button mapping written, one button per line (or under
.BR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME ).
.B ajazzctl
changes one button at a time but always sends all six, so this file must match
the mouse; delete it and run
.B buttons reset
if in doubt.
.TP
.I /dev/hidraw*
The mouse's vendor interface.
The installed udev rule gives the logged-in user access.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Mouse not found, no reply, or the read-back did not match.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command or argument.
.SH NOTES
Firmware updates are not supported and never sent.
The protocol is described in
.I docs/PROTOCOL.md
in the source repository.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ajazz_daemon ,
which writes the battery level to
.I /tmp/ajazz_battery
for status bars.
.PP
https://github.com/Rockeyxx/aj179-linux
